00:00:06.387 --> 00:00:08.720
SPEAKER: Container Registry
is a private container image

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.180
registry that runs
on Google Cloud.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.640
This quickstart shows you
how to build a Docker image

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.890
and how to push and pull the
image from your project's

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.920
Container Registry, all
from the command line.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.440
Before you begin, make sure
that, one, you have a Google

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.350
Cloud project
selected; two, building

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.470
and the Container Registry
API are enabled; and three,

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:29.240
you have the Cloud
SDK and Docker

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.460
installed on your system.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.650
For this quickstart,
we'll use Docker

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:35.930
to create a container
image of a small Python web

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:39.083
app, which we will then
push to Container Registry.

00:00:39.083 --> 00:00:40.500
To create the
Docker image, create

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.570
a directory with three files.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.390
One, the Docker file,
which provides information

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.040
on how to package
the application.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.030
Two, requirements.txt is
used to list your application

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.310
and dependencies.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:55.290
Three, app.py is the
actual Python code

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:56.832
of your application.

00:00:56.832 --> 00:00:59.040
Run the Docker Build command
to create a Docker image

00:00:59.040 --> 00:00:59.957
on your local machine.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.860
Next, we'll configure Docker
to use the gcloud command line

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.530
tool to authenticate requests
to Container Registry using

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.290
the gcloud auth command.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:14.860
Now we'll use the Docker Tag
command to associate the Docker

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.410
image with a registry name
so that we can push the image

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.843
to a specific location.

00:01:19.843 --> 00:01:21.260
We can now upload
the Docker image

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.400
to Container Registry with
the Docker Push command.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.600
You can view images hosted
by Container Registry

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.190
via the Google Cloud
console or by visiting

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:36.070
the image's registry
name in your web browser.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:37.990
To pull the image from
Container Registry

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:42.570
onto your local machine,
run the Docker Pull command.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.420
Finally, you can
delete the Docker image

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.360
from Container Registry using
the gcloud container images

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:48.050
Delete command.

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:51.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

